WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.667
TypeScript checks relationships between
types while

2
00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:05.014
code is being developed and compiled.

3
00:00:05.014 --> 00:00:08.174
Interfaces, type aliases, generic
parameters, and type assertions

4
00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:11.604
do not become input validators in the
emitted JavaScript.

5
00:00:11.604 --> 00:00:14.493
A request body asserted to be a create

6
00:00:14.493 --> 00:00:17.472
user request can still contain missing
fields, unexpected

7
00:00:17.472 --> 00:00:20.632
arrays, or incompatible values when the
service runs.

8
00:00:20.632 --> 00:00:23.611
The assertion supplies an assumption to
the compiler;

9
00:00:23.611 --> 00:00:26.680
it supplies no evidence about the sender's
data.

10
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:31.194
A typed function parameter has the same
limitation at an external boundary.

11
00:00:31.194 --> 00:00:34.805
Calling a function that accepts an account
update does not

12
00:00:34.805 --> 00:00:38.236
cause JavaScript to inspect the argument
against that type.

13
00:00:38.236 --> 00:00:41.486
A generic decoding helper is equally
incomplete if its

14
00:00:41.486 --> 00:00:44.916
implementation simply parses JSON and
asserts the requested type.

15
00:00:44.916 --> 00:00:47.444
Its convenient return signature can hide
the

16
00:00:47.444 --> 00:00:50.423
absence of checks from every caller,
spreading the

17
00:00:50.423 --> 00:00:53.131
initial assumption through otherwise
carefully typed code.

18
00:00:53.131 --> 00:00:55.840
Represent unverified values as unknown
where possible.

19
00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:59.451
That choice prevents ordinary property
access until the program narrows

20
00:00:59.451 --> 00:01:02.972
the value, making missing validation more
visible during development.

21
00:01:02.972 --> 00:01:05.138
It is a discipline enforced by

22
00:01:05.138 --> 00:01:07.576
the compiler, not automatic runtime
protection.

23
00:01:07.576 --> 00:01:10.284
An unchecked assertion can still bypass
it.

24
00:01:10.284 --> 00:01:13.624
By contrast, any permits operations
without useful checking and

25
00:01:13.624 --> 00:01:17.055
can make the boundary difficult to locate
during review.

26
00:01:17.055 --> 00:01:19.944
Keep raw values confined to a small
adapter

27
00:01:19.944 --> 00:01:22.652
rather than passing them throughout the
service.

28
00:01:22.652 --> 00:01:24.638
Runtime schemas provide executable checks.

29
00:01:24.638 --> 00:01:27.708
TypeScript applications use libraries such
as Zod, Valibot,

30
00:01:27.708 --> 00:01:30.326
and Joi, JSON Schema validators such as

31
00:01:30.326 --> 00:01:33.576
Ajv, framework validation, or carefully
scoped custom checks.

32
00:01:33.576 --> 00:01:35.832
Their APIs and defaults differ, especially

33
00:01:35.832 --> 00:01:37.819
for coercion and unknown properties.

34
00:01:37.819 --> 00:01:40.888
Choose explicit behavior and inspect the
accepted output.

35
00:01:40.888 --> 00:01:43.867
Derive application input types from
schemas when supported,

36
00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:46.936
or maintain tests that keep separate
definitions aligned.

37
00:01:46.936 --> 00:01:49.464
The internal type should describe what
validation

38
00:01:49.464 --> 00:01:52.263
actually returns, including optional
fields and transformations.

39
00:01:52.263 --> 00:01:55.152
A schema and an interface that disagree
create

40
00:01:55.152 --> 00:01:57.860
two competing contracts at the same
boundary.

